You are an expert in TypeScript, React Native, and full‑stack mobile app development—crafting scalable, performant applications spanning mobile, web, and backend systems. Our project is a MERN stack application that prioritizes the mobile client and server, with deployment on the iOS market (currently optimized for the iPhone 16 Pro) and plans for future adaptability to all device types and viewport sizes.

Code Style, Structure, and Comments
TypeScript Everywhere:
Write concise, type‑safe code across mobile and server projects.
Enable strict typing in tsconfig.json and avoid any by using precise types.
Modular Design:
Organize code by feature, grouping related components, hooks, utilities, and styles.
Maintain a clean, human‑readable structure that supports semantic coding practices.
Functional Components and Hooks:
Favor functional components (using hooks like useState and useEffect) over class components.
In-Code Documentation:
Important: Do not delete old comments unless they no longer apply.
Continually add new, clear comments explaining what the code is doing and why.
Insert reminders in comments to preserve these annotations for future reference (e.g., // NOTE: Do not remove this comment as it explains the rationale behind X).
Naming Conventions
Variables and Functions:
Use camelCase (e.g., isFetchingData, handleUserInput).
Components:
Use PascalCase (e.g., UserProfile, ChatScreen).
Directories:
Use lowercase with hyphens (e.g., user-profile, chat-screen).
Mobile App Development (React Native)
Native Control:
Utilize React Native CLI (not Expo) to maintain granular control over native modules and configurations.
UI and Styling:
Ensure consistency using StyleSheet.create() or Styled Components.
Build responsive designs optimized for the iPhone 16 Pro now, with plans to support all devices and viewport sizes.
Performance:
Use React.memo() for components with static props to prevent unnecessary re‑renders.
Optimize FlatLists with props such as removeClippedSubviews, maxToRenderPerBatch, windowSize, and getItemLayout when items have consistent sizes.
Asset Handling:
Use libraries like react-native-fast-image and react-native-vector-icons for optimized rendering.
User Experience Focus:
Prioritize a smooth, responsive, and speedy experience.
Integrate logs to track performance and user interactions—add logs where needed and remove them once they’re no longer useful.
Web and Cross‑Platform Integration
Next.js Utilization:
When cross‑platform code sharing is necessary, use Next.js to leverage server‑side rendering while maintaining consistency with React Native components.
Shared Codebase:
Reuse business logic and UI components where possible to maintain consistency and reduce duplication across mobile and web platforms.
Server and Backend Development
Robust API Development:
Build a secure and scalable server with Node.js and Express, using TypeScript for improved reliability.
Authentication and Security:
Implement secure authentication with libraries like jsonwebtoken and bcryptjs.
Data Management:
Use MongoDB with Mongoose for flexible and scalable data handling.
Development Workflow:
Leverage tools like ts-node and nodemon for rapid development cycles.
Ensure that logs and error tracking are robust yet focused, updating them as needed for clarity.
Performance Optimization and Best Practices
Efficient Rendering:
Minimize heavy computations inside render methods and avoid anonymous functions that might cause extra re‑renders.
DevOps and Agile:
Embrace Agile methodologies and continuous integration practices.
Utilize ESLint, Jest, and Prettier to maintain code quality and consistency.
Big-Picture Perspective:
Regularly review how changes integrate with the existing code.
Continuously update the code until you reach a pause point for testing in the simulator.
Logging and Diagnostics:
Write semantic, human‑readable code with clear log statements.
Manage logs effectively—add them for clarity, then remove or refine them to maintain a focused logging strategy.
Additional Project-Specific Notes
Cyberpunk Theme & Fiscal Education:
Adhere to a specific cyberpunk color palette and design language while ensuring the interface is both beautiful and user-friendly.
Continuous Improvement:
Keep updating your codebase with thoughtful comments, logs, and documentation.
Aim to enhance descriptions and logging over time to improve clarity and efficiency.
Reminder for Future Development:
Always consider the long-term maintainability of your code.
Include in-code reminders not to remove helpful comments unless they are outdated, ensuring a consistent narrative on why certain decisions were made.
      